WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
When you start learning a new language,
have you ever done the following? You've

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.189
gone to the local bookstore and bought a
bunch of language learning materials, you

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:13.259
have started surfing on the internet
trying to find every sort of language

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:16.680
platform you could find, you have
downloaded a bunch of apps on your phone..

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:23.609
Well don't do that ever again hi my name
is Luca from LucaLampariello.com and

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:28.289
today we're going to talk about how to
choose that one language learning

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:32.570
resource that is going to make all the
difference.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.760
Have you ever heard of the paradox of
choice?

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:42.559
Well, the paradox of choice is when we
have so much that we cannot make a

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:48.320
decision. That happens to me every day,
for example when I try to watch Netflix,

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.780
that is, I start looking for something to
look for, there's so much stuff that

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:58.970
I end up spending more time looking for
things to watch than actually watching

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:03.379
this stuff. This has happened a number of
times and the only solution is to think

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:08.270
beforehand before hopping on Netflix. But
the problem still stands, and it still

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.979
stands for a number of things, language
learning included. I still remember that

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:18.799
some 25 years ago, when I started
learning German,I just found the course De

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:22.970
Agostini Il Tedesco per te (German for you) and
that's what I used for one year and a

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:28.220
half before moving on to another
resource. So I beg you, if you have just

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.299
started learning a language or if you
want to start learning a language, make

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:38.060
sure that you're going to choose one and
only one resource and use it in order to

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:47.090
build a very solid foundation in your
target language. Ok. Now that you know

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.890
that you have to use just one resource,
how do you go about choosing that one

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:56.900
resource? Well, I think there are three
main features to factor in when it comes

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:01.670
to choosing a good language learning
material. First, your language learning

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.780
material has to be comprehensible. What
does it mean that a language learning

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.649
resource is comprehensible?
Well it's comprehensible when its

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:15.080
content allows you to understand what
you're reading and what you're listening

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.200
to. So on the one hand, the content should

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.000
contain obviously the target language
but possibly it could also contain

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.549
another version, the same version in your
native language or in a language you

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:33.200
speak well so that you can use that
language, you can leverage that knowledge

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.560
in order to understand and dissect your
target language. But the resource doesn't

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:45.049
necessarily have to be just bilingual, it
could be monolingual but if that is the

00:02:45.049 --> 00:02:48.500
case, it has to be extremely clear and it has

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.700
to explain things so that you can
understand it in that target language

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.920
and third, there could be a resource that
has content mainly in your target

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.420
language but also a vocab section in
your own native language or some

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:07.400
explanation in your own native language
of its grammar features. The second

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:13.010
factor is that that language resource
should be enjoyable. If it's an app,

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:19.610
whether it's an app, a book or a website
you should like its content visually, you

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.930
should like spending time looking at
that resource in terms of visual content

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.670
and on the other side you should like
spending time listening to the audio of

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:33.739
that resource. Clear slow audio is very
important at the very beginning. And

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:39.709
third, that resource that you're using
should be useful, that is, the content

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:44.660
should be aimed at the skill you want to
develop. So for example if that language

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:50.780
learning resource had real everyday
dialogues in your target language that's

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.010
great if you want to speak that language
well and apply it to everyday life

00:03:55.010 --> 00:04:00.350
instead of, say, having very long
descriptive texts on the geography of

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:05.299
the country where your target language
is spoken or history or other things

00:04:05.299 --> 00:04:11.769
that you can actually do and you can
learn a little bit later down the line.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:19.890
Imagine a hammer. With this hammer you can 
kill someone or you can build something

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:25.990
you can kill or you can create. No matter
the quality of the hammer, it's what you

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.620
ultimately do with it that makes a
difference. A language learning material

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.330
is a little bit like that hammer. Most
language learning materials are

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:37.750
relatively good in terms of content but
ultimately the real good language

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:43.120
learners know how to use these resources. 
Nowadays most language learning

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.560
resources are actually already pretty
good but what really makes a difference

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:53.860
is how a learner uses them. Now, get this:
the education system all around the

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:59.670
world over the last 200 years has been
hinging on the acquisition of knowledge

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:05.830
instead and to the detriment of learning
a skill. This endemic form of poor

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:11.200
training applies to a lot of fields,
ranging from mathematics to physics and

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.270
obviously to language learning. Have you
ever wondered why after majoring in, say,

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:20.890
Spanish for five years, you have realized
that you can't actually string a

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:25.210
sentence together in that language even
if you know a lot of words? Come to think

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:30.730
of it, language learning is a combination
of two very important skills, the

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:36.070
capacity of acquiring knowledge on the
one hand, that is, words and grammar rules

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:41.800
and the capacity of putting these words
together and that is forming correct

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:47.170
sentences in terms of grammar and
pronunciation. Now, kids do that very well

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.580
look at a five-year-old kid they can
already form perfectly grammatical

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:56.650
sentences in their own language but if
you ask them what is an adverb what is

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:01.090
an adjective
they have no clue because they have

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:07.060
learned that at school after learning to
put together sentences Adults to have

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:12.760
these two systems in place but they only
use one system and that is how we

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.740
acquire knowledge. Adults also have
exactly the same capacity that kids do

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:23.409
how do kids learn languages so well
without even knowing about

00:06:23.409 --> 00:06:30.399
grammar? Well, they combine knowledge, a little bit of knowledge, they know 500 to

00:06:30.399 --> 00:06:36.459
1000 words, a relatively short a
relatively small amount of vocab but

00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:41.559
they immediately train in forming
sentences, in combining these words

00:06:41.559 --> 00:06:47.679
together to form meaning and adults do
not do that. In formal education, we

00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:53.110
mostly focus on acquiring knowledge, we
spend more time speaking about the

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.489
language speaking about its features of
grammar patterns, its pronunciation

00:06:57.489 --> 00:07:03.189
patterns than actually speak it. So,
whatever resource you're going to use,

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:07.989
make sure that you don't read your resource as if it were a normal book, use it as a

00:07:07.989 --> 00:07:14.349
manual to build skills, just to give you
an example instead of making lists of

00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:20.019
words and trying to memorize them, make
sure that immediately you take these

00:07:20.019 --> 00:07:25.179
words in pairs or in groups of three and
you combine them in order to create

00:07:25.179 --> 00:07:30.639
short and meaningful sentences. Until the
age of 14 I had been chained to this

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:34.599
educational system, and I thought that
the best way to learn German was

00:07:34.599 --> 00:07:39.369
actually to try to memorize lists of
words and learn its grammar patterns but

00:07:39.369 --> 00:07:45.550
after struggling a few weeks with very
very boring grammar exercises I realized

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:51.360
that I could actually do something else. So I started using a translation method

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.369
translating from German into my native
language Italian and back a few days

00:07:57.369 --> 00:08:03.369
later in order to understand the
patterns of that language. That worked so

00:08:03.369 --> 00:08:09.189
well that I used that to learn German
and after one year I started learning

00:08:09.189 --> 00:08:13.689
Spanish and then I applied it to pretty
much every other language that I speak

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:18.550
right now. So to wrap up the content of
the video, first, make sure that you spend

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:23.229
some time doing some proper research to
find the one resource to start with and

00:08:23.229 --> 00:08:27.489
use that one resource for a few months
before moving onto another resource.

00:08:27.489 --> 00:08:34.240
Second, make sure that this resource is
comprehensible enjoyable and useful that

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.700
is aimed at developing the skill you
want to.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:41.770
develop from the very beginning and
third, make sure that you learn how to

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:47.110
use that resource to build skills and
sub-skills within the realm of language

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:50.500
learning. You should not just read through
that resource you should not just read

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.000
or listen to that resource you should do
things that are aimed at developing

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.410
skills - putting words together, learning
to up their entire sentences and

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:03.100
ultimately, to speak the language. So
there you have it. If you want to know

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:07.180
more please click on the link in the
description box, there's a deluge of

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:10.960
additional information about this
fantastic topic, thank you very much and

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.450
I'll see you very soon here on YouTube

